Now I see something important. In `Select::_interact_on()`, the loop structure is:

```rust
term.hide_cursor()?;
// ... setup ...
loop {
    // render
    term.flush()?;
    match term.read_key()? {
        // handle keys
    }
    // ... paging and clearing
    paging.update_page(sel);
    term.clear_last_lines(paging.capacity)?;
}
// ... after loop
term.clear_last_lines(paging.capacity)?;


term.show_cursor()?;
term.flush()?;
```

When `term.read_key()` returns an error from Ctrl+D, the `?` operator propagates it up and the function exits without ever reaching `term.show_cursor()`, leaving the cursor hidden. This is a resource leak. Let me trace through the rest of the `_interact_on` method to see if there are other error paths that could cause similar issues.

